{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are consistently too high vs. realized prices (often by ~2–10 EUR), especially after the first week; apply a downward level shift to the entire path.",
    "Underestimates downside momentum: when the recent history shows a rollover/decline, the model projects stabilization or rebound too early; enforce stronger continuation of the prevailing downtrend for ~10–20 days.",
    "Mean-reversion assumption too strong: the model pulls prices back toward the recent average, but realized outcomes continue drifting lower; reduce mean-reversion strength and increase trend-following weight in bearish regimes.",
    "Overshoot on late-horizon rebounds: the model frequently introduces a rising tail (days ~20–30) that does not materialize; dampen late-horizon upward slopes and cap rebound magnitude after drawdowns.",
    "Volatility/compression mismatch: the model path is smoother and higher; increase sensitivity to negative shocks and allow lower lows (wider downside distribution) rather than compressing around mid-70s."
  ]
}
